    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d27">

                <pb facs="0104" n="24" sameAs="frus:frus1901/pg24"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bridgman to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">La
                                Paz, Bolivia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-05-20"><hi rendition="#italic">May 20,
                        1901</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 265.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose herewith copy
                    of a letter from George Melville, an Englishman who has been imprisoned at
                    Corocoro for more than two months, accused of robbery.</p>
                <p>As illustrating the delays mentioned in another dispatch, this case is a good
                    example. Several letters of the character inclosed have been sent to this
                    legation, and official and personal appeals made to bring Melville to trial.</p>
                <p>One main trouble is disregard of commands from the foreign office by the local
                    authorities at Corocoro. I can not determine as to the probability of this man’s
                    guilt or innocence, but believe he states the facts in his letter as
                    existing.</p>
                <p>Similar cases are not infrequent here, and as no provision exists for relief are
                    quite distressing.</p>
                <p>I am sending to-day a second request to his accuser, asking his influence to
                    bring Melville to trial.</p>
                <p>Trusting I may be able to report definitely before long,</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George H.
                    Bridgman.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d27-a1.xml" xpointer="d27-a1"/>
